WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.093 --> 00:00:09.225
Late the following afternoon, Mr. Havisham came for Cedric in the earl's carriage.

2
00:00:09.226 --> 00:00:14.280
Cedric was sad to leave his mother.

3
00:00:14.281 --> 00:00:20.566
But when he went outside, he was immediately fascinated by the carriage.

4
00:00:20.567 --> 00:00:26.664
"Did you ever see such beautiful horses?" Cedric asked Mr. Havisham.

5
00:00:26.665 --> 00:00:34.245
"The earl's stable is full of magnificent horses," the lawyer replied.

6
00:00:34.246 --> 00:00:38.041
Cedric squinted at a design on the door.

7
00:00:38.042 --> 00:00:44.811
"That little crown with the big D above it—is that a coronet?"

8
00:00:44.812 --> 00:00:51.162
"Yes, and the D stands for Dorincourt."

9
00:00:51.163 --> 00:00:58.788
"Oh, that's so people realize the earl is inside." Cedric looked at Mr. Havisham.

10
00:00:58.789 --> 00:01:02.630
"Or somebody the earl knows—like you."

11
00:01:02.631 --> 00:01:10.200
"Or you." Mr. Havisham gave a curt nod.

12
00:01:10.201 --> 00:01:13.637
A man in uniform held open the door.

13
00:01:13.638 --> 00:01:18.868
"Mr. Havisham, who's that?" Cedric whispered.

14
00:01:18.869 --> 00:01:25.132
"A servant called a footman. There are more footmen at the castle.

15
00:01:25.133 --> 00:01:36.034
Now hurry up and get in. You don't want to be late for your first meeting with your grandfather."

16
00:01:36.035 --> 00:01:42.252
It took only a few minutes to reach the gate of the great park surrounding the castle.

17
00:01:42.253 --> 00:01:49.075
Cedric peered out the window at a pair of stone lions guarding the entrance.

18
00:01:49.076 --> 00:01:54.899
A woman and two boys came out of an ivy-covered cottage.

19
00:01:54.900 --> 00:02:00.000
While the mother opened the gate, the boys waved to Cedric.

20
00:02:00.001 --> 00:02:04.157
Then their mother curtseyed and smiled at him.

21
00:02:04.158 --> 00:02:10.658
"I don't know those people," Cedric said, "but they act like they know me."

22
00:02:10.659 --> 00:02:17.447
"They know you're Lord Fauntleroy," Mr. Havisham explained.

23
00:02:17.448 --> 00:02:24.960
Cedric took off his cap and waved. "Good afternoon!" he called out.

24
00:02:24.961 --> 00:02:34.234
The woman's smile grew even broader. "Welcome, your lordship!"

25
00:02:34.235 --> 00:02:40.384
Cedric turned to Mr. Havisham. "That woman looks as if she likes children.

26
00:02:40.385 --> 00:02:44.619
I'd enjoy coming here to play with her sons."

27
00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:50.856
Mr. Havisham didn't tell him that a young lord doesn't play with a gatekeeper's children.

28
00:02:50.857 --> 00:02:55.956
There was plenty of time for Cedric to learn the rules of his new life.

29
00:02:55.957 --> 00:03:01.457
The carriage rolled down a broad avenue with tall trees on either side.

30
00:03:01.458 --> 00:03:06.150
It passed masses of ferns and flowers.

31
00:03:06.151 --> 00:03:09.595
Cedric pointed excitedly out the window.

32
00:03:09.596 --> 00:03:17.333
"There's a whole herd of deer! Who do they belong to?"

33
00:03:17.334 --> 00:03:25.868
Mr. Havisham smiled at Cedric's delight. "The earl—and one day, you."

34
00:03:25.869 --> 00:03:30.464
"What would Mr. Hobbs think of that?" Cedric said.

35
00:03:30.465 --> 00:03:37.524
"This park is bigger than Central Park in New York. But where's the castle?"

36
00:03:37.525 --> 00:03:40.686
"Several miles away."

37
00:03:40.687 --> 00:03:44.285
Cedric settled back against the seat cushions.

38
00:03:44.286 --> 00:03:52.098
"That's a long way for somebody to live from his front gate."

39
00:03:52.099 --> 00:03:58.282
As the castle came into view, the setting sun turned its windows to gold.

40
00:03:58.283 --> 00:04:10.169
"Ohh." Cedric sighed. "It's just like a king's palace in a fairy tale."

41
00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:15.801
Inside the castle a line of servants was waiting to greet Cedric.

42
00:04:15.802 --> 00:04:21.010
At the front of the line stood a kind-looking woman.

43
00:04:21.011 --> 00:04:29.507
"Lord Fauntleroy," Mr. Havisham said, "this is Mrs. Mellon, the housekeeper."

44
00:04:29.508 --> 00:04:35.565
Cedric's eyes lit up. "Thank you for giving the cat to my mother."

45
00:04:35.566 --> 00:04:42.168
Mrs. Mellon looked pleased. "The cat left two fine kittens here.

46
00:04:42.169 --> 00:04:46.487
I'll send them up to your lordship's rooms."

47
00:04:46.488 --> 00:04:50.552
Mr. Havisham said something to her in a low voice.

48
00:04:50.553 --> 00:05:00.800
"In the library, sir," she replied. "I've been instructed that his lordship should enter the room alone."

49
00:05:00.801 --> 00:05:07.741
Cedric looked around with great interest as a footman escorted him to the library.

50
00:05:07.742 --> 00:05:16.085
The footman opened the door and announced, "Lord Fauntleroy."

51
00:05:16.086 --> 00:05:21.188
Cedric entered a splendid room filled with books and heavy furniture.

52
00:05:21.189 --> 00:05:25.604
The room was so dark that Cedric thought no one was there.

53
00:05:25.605 --> 00:05:29.714
Then he saw someone sitting in the easy chair by the fire.

54
00:05:29.715 --> 00:05:33.179
The man didn't bother to look up.

55
00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:41.832
But the huge dog lying by the fire noticed the boy and walked over to him.

56
00:05:41.833 --> 00:05:48.189
The man in the chair spoke to the dog. "Come, Dougal."

57
00:05:48.190 --> 00:05:55.926
Cedric put his hand on the dog's collar. Together they approached the man.

58
00:05:55.933 --> 00:06:00.673
"Are you the earl? I'm your grandson, Cedric.

59
00:06:00.674 --> 00:06:04.342
Mr. Havisham brought me from New York."

60
00:06:04.343 --> 00:06:11.635
Cedric held out his hand. "I hope you're well. I'm glad to meet you."

61
00:06:11.636 --> 00:06:15.395
The earl was surprised by Cedric's good manners.

62
00:06:15.396 --> 00:06:21.683
It pleased the grim, old nobleman that Cedric wasn't afraid of the dog or himself.

63
00:06:21.684 --> 00:06:27.791
"Glad to meet me, are you?" the earl said as they shook hands.

64
00:06:27.792 --> 00:06:34.651
"Of course," Cedric replied. "Everyone is glad to meet their relatives."

65
00:06:34.652 --> 00:06:37.952
The earl thought about his own family.

66
00:06:37.953 --> 00:06:44.034
He'd spent his life quarreling with his relatives and throwing them out of his house.

67
00:06:44.035 --> 00:06:52.932
They all despised him. In fact, 35 years had passed since he'd last seen his only sister.

68
00:06:52.933 --> 00:07:02.116
Cedric took a seat near the earl. "Any boy would love his grandfather, especially one who's been so kind."

69
00:07:02.117 --> 00:07:09.525
The earl was startled. "What do you mean?" he demanded.

